  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\n   We use a randomized local search algorithm that iteratively explores the space of step functions to minimize the overlap integral. The key intuition is to start with a good initial point and then perform local searches with geometric variations. This approach is chosen for its ability to find locally optimal solutions, but we employ randomized restarts to escape local minima.\n\n   We define the step function as a piecewise constant function with a fixed total integral. The search is guided by a smooth surrogate objective that penalizes large correlations at various lags. The algorithm maintains the global best solution found and uses simulated annealing to allow occasional uphill moves.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n   Variables: \n     - A step function h(x) over [0,2] represented by m points (allowing flexibility)\n     - Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n     - Objective: min max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (over all real shifts k)\n\n   The evaluation uses:\n     - Grid discretization with n_point samples\n     - dx = 2/n_points\n     - Correlation-based overlap: max_k [dx * np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')]\n\n   We use a smooth surrogate objective: \n     f(x) = -E_{l} [log(sum_{i} e^{\u03b1(c_i(x) - c_0^*(x))} + \u03b2|\u222bh(x)dx - 1| \n\n   Where:\n     - c_i(x) is the i-th lag correlation\n     - c_0^*(x) is the maximum lag\n     - \u03b1 and \u03b2 are tuning parameters\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\n   a) Initialization:\n      - Start with provided `initial_h_values` (if available)\n      - Project the initial solution to valid [0,1] space preserving sum\n\n   b) Setup:\n      - Set grid size n_points (300)\n      - Define initial point h_initial with n_points samples\n\n   c) Local Search:\n      - Perturb current point by adding Gaussian noise (scale \u03c3)\n      - Project new point to feasible set\n      - Evaluate by computing correlation via:\n            correlation = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')\n            scale = dx = 2.0/n_points\n            c5 = max(correlation)*scale\n      - Accept new solution if c5 is improved or with probability exp(-\u0394c5/T) (simulated annealing)\n\n   d) Restart Policy:\n      - After 100 evaluations, restart from best point so far\n      - Exponentially decay temperature T\n\n   e) Escape Mechanism:\n      - When stuck in local minimum, perform large perturbation\n\n4. Search Choices:\n\n   a) Parameters:\n      - Initial temperature T0 = 0.5\n      - Cooling rate r = 0.995\n      - Perturbation scale \u03c3 = 0.001 (initial)\n      - Anneal temperature decay: \u03c3 *= 0.98\n\n   b) Neighborhood:\n      - Add Gaussian noise to log-scaled parameters (for systematicity)\n      - Max evaluations per run: 5000\n      - Max temperature evolution steps: 1000\n\n   c) Monitoring:\n      - Track best solution overall\n      - Return best found solution\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\n   a) Scheduling:\n      - Total evaluations: budget_s / (dx computation + projection)\n      - Time per evaluation: mainly n_points of correlations (O(n^2))\n      - We use 300 grid points (dx = ~0.0067)\n\n   b) Deadline Handling:\n      - Monitor time periodically\n      - Immediately return best solution when time < margin\n\n   c) Validation:\n      - Check bounds and integral\n      - Compute final correlation\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n   a) Feasibility:\n      - All h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n      - sum(h)*dx \u2248 1.0 (within 1e-6)\n      - Returned h has n_points samples\n\n   b) Objective Assessment:\n      - Compute c5 = max_k [dx * np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')]\n\n   c) Output Format:\n      - (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where n_points = len(h_values)",
